For progressive muscle relaxation, a commonly used method is the 16-muscle group technique, which involves systematically tensing and relaxing different muscle groups in the body. This can include the toes, feet, calves, thighs, hips, back, shoulders, arms, hands, neck, and head. A typical sequence might start with the toes and move up to the head, holding each muscle group for 5-10 seconds before releasing. It's essential to focus on deep, slow breathing during the exercise to enhance relaxation.

When using progressive muscle relaxation during a headache episode, you might find it helpful to start with shorter sessions, focusing on the most tense areas, such as the neck and shoulders. You can also try adapting the technique to your specific needs, such as focusing on the muscles in your temples or forehead. To make it easier during an episode, try to find a quiet, comfortable space to sit or lie down, close your eyes, and concentrate on your breathing. If you find that the headache worsens or you experience any increase in discomfort, it's best to stop the exercise and consult with a healthcare professional for further guidance.

