When setting stricter benchmarks for farmers, you can gauge the tolerable loss of suppliers by considering the impact on your supply chain, the quality of the remaining suppliers, and the potential for finding alternative suppliers. A framework to measure the trade-off could include assessing the number of suppliers that would be lost, the volume of produce that would be affected, and the potential increase in quality and consistency. If the loss of suppliers would not significantly disrupt the supply chain and the remaining suppliers can meet the required standards, it may be a tolerable loss. However, if the loss would severely impact the supply chain or compromise the quality of the produce, it may be necessary to reconsider the stricter benchmarks.

